      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-141479",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the polar curve $r = \\sin^{2} \u03b8$ for $\u03b8 \u2208 (-\\frac{\u03c0}{2}, \\frac{\u03c0}{2})$. Given that\n$x^{\u2032}(\u03b8) = 2\\sin \u03b8\\cos^{2} \u03b8 - \\sin^{3} \u03b8, y^{\u2032}(\u03b8) = 3\\sin^{2} \u03b8\\cos \u03b8$,\nand $y^{\u2032}(\u03b8) = 0$ at $\u03b8 = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x^{\u2032}(0) = 0$\n2. $lim_(\u03b8 \u2192 0)\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\u03b8 = 0$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
    },
