      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-143555",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the polar curve $r = 1 - \\sin \u03b8$ for $\u03b8 \u2208 [0, 2\u03c0)$. Given that\n\n$x^{\u2032}(\u03b8) =-\\sin (\u03b8) - \\cos (2\u03b8), y^{\u2032}(\u03b8) = \\cos (\u03b8) - \\sin (2\u03b8)$,\nand $x^{\u2032}(\\frac{\u03c0}{2}) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{\u2032}(\\frac{\u03c0}{2}) = 0$\n2. $lim_(\u03b8 \u2192 \u03c0/2)\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\u03b8 = \u03c0/2$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
